<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gyeongju</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Seorabeol, Geumseong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gyeongju is a city in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> that was the capital of Silla for nearly a thousand years, until 935. It is rich in royal tombs, temples and palace sites, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulguksa">Bulguksa</a> temple, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seokguram">Seokguram</a> grotto and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheomseongdae">Cheomseongdae</a> observatory, and its historic areas are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. It is often called a museum without walls.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gyeongju.jpg" alt="Gyeongju" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kyoww (montage) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gyeongju_montage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
